      <button class="btn-primary" onclick="window.print()">Print recipe</button>
      <span class="muted" style="margin-left: auto; font-size: 0.88rem;" id="recipeCounter">Showing recipe 1 of 60</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Card 2: Part 1 — First Fermentation -->
  <section class="card">
    <h2>Part 1 — First fermentation (plain water kefir)</h2>
    <p class="muted">Equipment: 3-cup glass mason jar and plastic or nylon mesh strainer.</p>

    <div class="ingredients">
      <div class="ingredient">
        <div class="ingredient-label">Filtered or spring water</div>
        <div class="amount-display">
          <span class="amount-val">600</span>
          <span class="unit">g</span>
        </div>
        <div class="approx">About 2.5 cups</div>
        <div class="prep-note">Room-temperature, non-chlorinated water. Fixed jar size remains 3 cups.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="ingredient">
        <div class="ingredient-label">Fermentation sugar</div>
        <div class="amount-display">
          <span class="amount-val">33</span>
          <span class="unit">g</span>
        </div>
        <div class="approx">About 2.5 tbsp (30 to 35 g)</div>
        <div class="prep-note">Demerara, organic cane, or turbinado sugar.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="ingredient">
        <div class="ingredient-label">Active water kefir grains</div>
        <div class="amount-display">
          <span class="amount-val">30–40</span>
          <span class="unit">g</span>
        </div>
        <div class="approx">About 2 to 2.5 tablespoons</div>
        <div class="prep-note">Healthy, plump rehydrated grains.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="yield-box">
      <strong>Yield note:</strong> This 600 g first-fermentation batch yields enough strained plain water kefir for exactly <strong>two 16-oz bottles (~300 g each)</strong>. The amounts in Part 2 below are listed per individual 16-oz bottle.
    </div>

    <ol>
      <li><strong>Dissolve the sugar:</strong> Dissolve 33 g (about 2.5 tbsp) sugar in a small amount of warm water in the 3-cup jar. Add the remaining water and let it cool completely to room temperature. Never add grains to warm water.</li>
      <li><strong>Add the grains:</strong> Add your rehydrated water kefir grains to the clean 3-cup jar and pour in the cooled sugar water. Leave about 0.5 cups of headspace at the top.</li>
      <li><strong>Cover loosely:</strong> Cover the jar loosely with a coffee filter, paper towel, or breathable cloth secured with a rubber band.</li>
      <li><strong>Primary ferment:</strong> Let it ferment out of direct sunlight at room temperature (68–78 deg F) for about 24 to 48 hours. It is ready when mildly sweet and slightly tangy.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- Card 3: Part 2 — Second Fermentation -->
  <section class="card" id="part2Section">
    <h2>Part 2 — Second fermentation (flavor and carbonation)</h2>
    <p class="muted">Equipment: pressure-rated 16 oz (1-pint) swing-top bottles and a kitchen scale. Amounts below are the exact fixed amounts for each 16 oz bottle; about 300 g plain water kefir is the liquid amount.</p>

    <div class="recipe-header-block">
      <div class="tag-row">
        <span class="tag" id="recipeCatTag">Strawberry & Berry Flavors</span>
        <span class="tag tag-speed" id="recipeSpeedTag">Fast carbonation</span>
      </div>
      <h3 class="recipe-title" id="recipeTitle">Strawberry Lemonade Water Kefir</h3>
      <p class="muted" id="recipeDesc">A bright sweet-tart classic.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Exact flavor ingredients per 16 oz bottle:</h3>
    <div class="ingredients" id="flavorIngredients"></div>

    <div class="ingredient" style="margin-bottom: 14px;">
      <div class="ingredient-label">Strained plain water kefir (from Part 1)</div>
      <div class="amount-display">
        <span class="amount-val">300</span>
        <span class="unit">g</span>
      </div>
      <div class="approx">About 1.25 cups (~300 grams per 16 oz bottle)</div>
      <div class="prep-note">Keep 1 to 2 inches of headspace at the top of the bottle; do not fill to the brim.</div>
    </div>

    <h3>How to do it:</h3>
    <ol id="recipeSteps"></ol>

    <div class="tip-box" id="recipeTipBox">
      <strong>Flavor tip:</strong> <span id="recipeTipText"></span>
    </div>

    <div class="note">
      <strong>Pressure safety:</strong> Fermenting fruit sugars, juices, and residual sugar build carbonation quickly. Use only bottles specifically rated for pressure and carbonation, inspect them for cracks, leave 1 to 2 inches of headspace, check them daily, and refrigerate thoroughly before opening. Burping vents excess pressure but does not guarantee against over-pressurization. Refrigeration slows fermentation down significantly. Always open chilled bottles carefully, directed away from your face.
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Card 4: Part 3 — Keep the Cycle Going -->
  <section class="card">
    <h2>Part 3 — Keep the cycle going</h2>
    <p class="muted">Water kefir grains are reusable indefinitely if fed regularly. You do not need to rehydrate them again.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Continuous brewing cycle:</strong> As soon as your first fermentation finishes, strain out the grains with your plastic or nylon mesh strainer and place them right back into a 3-cup mason jar filled with fresh room-temperature sugar water (600 g water + 33 g sugar). Repeat every 24 to 48 hours (about every 2 days).</li>
      <li><strong>Taking a short break (up to 3 weeks):</strong> Place the grains in a 3-cup jar with fresh sugar water, secure with a solid airtight lid (instead of a cloth), and store them in the refrigerator. When you are ready to brew again, strain them out and start a fresh batch at room temperature.</li>
      <li><strong>Cleanliness and grain health:</strong> Use clean non-reactive equipment, avoid metal contact with grains, and discard a batch if you ever see colorful fuzzy mold or notice an unusual rotten smell.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- Card 5: Flavor Directory Included -->
  <section class="card" id="flavorListSection">
    <h2>Flavor ideas included</h2>
    <p class="muted">Click any flavor below to load its exact ingredients and step-by-step instructions above.</p>
    <div class="flavor-list-grid" id="flavorListGrid"></div>
  </section>
</main>

<footer>
  Homemade water kefir recipe helper &middot; Fixed 3-cup first-fermentation jar &middot; Fixed 16 oz flavor bottle &middot; 60 pre-calibrated flavor formulations
